-- title:
--   Formal smoothness of the generic fibre of the blow-up chart
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q\ge 5$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell\ge 3$ with $\ell\ne q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$.
--
--   **Base field and root of unity.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\xi\in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and `hι` requires the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$.
--
--   **Function field.** $K$ is an intermediate field of $L\subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, and `hK` pins it down as $K=\mathrm{laurentBaseChange}\,L$ applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79): that is, $K$ is the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) induced by $\mathbb Q\to L$, of the $q$-expansion function field over $\mathbb Q$ of level $(q\ell)^2M'$ for the subgroup $H=\ker\big((\mathbb Z/(q\ell)^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell)^\times\big)$.
--
--   **Coefficient ring.** $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, Henselian local, with algebraically closed residue field, made into an algebra over which $K$ is a tower ($A\to L\to K$); $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$, and $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$. Furthermore $\varpi\in A$ generates the maximal ideal, and $\varpi_t\in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$.
--
--   **The coordinate $j$.** $j\in K$ is nonzero and its Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the image of the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}\cdot(\text{power series})$ of the modular $j$-function. Write $C=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the finite chart algebra of the two-chart integral model of $K$ over $A$ with parameter $j$, and `jChartFin A ↥K j` for $j$ regarded as an element of $C$; $X=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) is the scheme obtained by gluing $\operatorname{Spec} C$ to the chart at infinity, `toBase` its structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and `ιFin` the morphism $\operatorname{Spec} C\to X$.
--
--   **A supersingular point.** $y$ is a maximal ideal of $C$ containing the image of $\varpi$, and the hypothesis `hss` requires: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of those $j$-invariants such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point in its affine point group.
--
--   **The corresponding point of the integral model.** $z$ is a point of $X$; $\varpi_z$ is defined by `hϖz` as the germ at $z$ of the global section of $X$ obtained from $\varpi$ through `toBase` and the canonical identification of $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} A)$ with $A$, and `hz` requires $\varpi_z$ to lie in the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal O_{X,z}$. Moreover $y'$ is a point of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with `ιFin` sending $y'$ to $z$, the hypothesis `hss'` imposes the same supersingularity condition as `hss` for the prime $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, and `hy'y` requires $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}=y$.
--
--   **Drinfeld form for the complete local ring at $z$.** $W_1$ is a discrete valuation domain, complete for its maximal-adic topology, $\sigma_1:A\to W_1$ a ring homomorphism such that the maximal ideal of $W_1$ is generated by $\sigma_1\varpi$; $f_1,u_1,v_1$ are two-variable power series over $W_1$ with $u_1,v_1$ units, and $f_1$ congruent to [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W₁`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18) $=X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$. Finally $e_1$ is a ring isomorphism from the maximal-adic completion $\widehat{\mathcal O}_{X,z}$ onto
--   $$S=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]\big/\big(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v_1-f_1u_1\big).$$
--
--   **The package `hW₁`.** With $\mathrm{STK}=\mathcal O_{X,z}$, $\mathrm{CMP}=\widehat{\mathcal O}_{X,z}$, $\mathrm{toC}$ the completion map, $\mathrm{mkS}$ the quotient map onto $S$, and $\mathrm{germY}:C\to\mathcal O_{X,z}$ the germ map at $z$ along `ιFin` (through the canonical identifications), `hW₁` is a conjunction of seven clauses, summarised here. (1) For every $a\in A$, $e_1$ carries the image of $a$ in $\widehat{\mathcal O}_{X,z}$ to the class of the constant $C(\sigma_1 a)$. (2) For every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau\in\mathrm{Aut}_L(K)$ with [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — i.e. $\tau$ realises on $K$ the action of $\gamma^{-1}$ on $q$-expansions: for all weights $k$ and all modular forms $f,g$ of level $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions, $g$ with nonzero series, and all $x\in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the ratio of those series, the expansion of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of the $\gamma^{-1}$-translate of $g$ equals that of the $\gamma^{-1}$-translate of $f$, under any embedding $\iota:L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$ — the automorphism $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$. (3) If in addition $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, the restriction of such a $\tau$ to $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$. (4) For $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, $\tau$ as above preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, there are $\theta\in\mathrm{Aut}(S)$, $c\in W_1$ and a $2\times 2$ matrix $M$ over $W_1$ such that $\theta$ intertwines $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$ with the action of $\tau$ on $C$, $\theta$ fixes all constants $C(w)$, $\theta(X_j)\equiv\sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0,X_1$, $c^{q+1}\equiv 1$ and $M_{ij}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W_1$, $c\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, and $c\not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$. (5) For integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each omitting at least one of the classes of $X_0,X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma_1\varpi)$, and each containing a linear form $a_iX_0+b_iX_1$ corrected by an element of $(X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to $\mathcal O_{X,z}$ along $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}$ are distinct. (6) For a prime $P$ of $S$ satisfying the same two conditions and containing $X_0$ corrected by an element of $(X_0,X_1)^2$, and for every $a\in C$: the image of $a$ in $\widehat{\mathcal O}_{X,z}$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e_1$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$. (7) The Laurent series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$; there are $a_0\in A$ with the difference of this element of $C$ and $a_0$ in $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, an integer $e_0\ge 1$ and $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that for all $a,b\in W_1$, not both in the maximal ideal, with $a^qb-ab^q$ in the maximal ideal, the sum $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0}\mathrm{coeff}_{(i,\,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit, and $e_1$ sends the image of that difference to the class of $h$.
--
--   **Centre and blow-up chart.** $J$ is an ideal of $C$, and `hJ` requires $J$ to be the infimum of the set of ideals $J'$ of $C$ of the following form: for some $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and some $\tau\in\mathrm{Aut}_L(K)$ with `IsLevelAutAt … γ⁻¹ K τ` mapping $C$ into $C$, $J'$ is the contraction along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ of the contraction, along $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$, of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. Finally $B$ is an $A$-subalgebra of $K$, and `hB` requires $B$ to be, after restriction of scalars to $A$, the $C$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by $\{x\in K:\exists\,i\in J,\ x\,\varpi_t=i\}$; that is, $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Let $\kappa(\bot)$ be `(⊥ : Ideal A).ResidueField`, the residue field of $A$ at its zero prime (a model of the fraction field of $A$). Then the fibre `(⊥ : Ideal A).Fiber ↥B`, the base change of $B$ along $A\to\kappa(\bot)$, is a formally smooth $\kappa(\bot)$-algebra.
--
--   This is the generic-fibre half of the smoothness statement for the weighted blow-up chart $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$ of the finite chart algebra along the centre $J$ cut out by the Drinfeld local model at the supersingular point $z$. It is combined with the corresponding assertion over the closed point to yield smoothness of the blow-up chart in [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.formallySmooth_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.formallySmooth_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness); the proof cites finite generation, normality and localisation properties of the chart algebras together with smoothness over a perfect field of a finite-type normal domain of Krull dimension at most one.
